I think so, if you take it apart, you will find something interesting:
"What gets wet when it dries?"
You used (get wet) to mention its state, to create a real conflict between 
the words you would use (gets dry) instead of (dries)... a bit weak 
explanation, I know, but that's what English allows me!
So, "What gets wet as it dries <something>" should mean that it takes the 
wetness out of something else, so I'd say that this isn't playing with words 
but may well be a "Lojbanic puzzle"!
PS. I'm ignoring it's a known trick here ;-)

